{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ממקר", :translit "mimkor", :gloss "from her source (defective spelling)"} {:he "מרקם", :translit "marekom", :gloss "their embroidery / variegated fabric"} {:he "וטהרה", :translit "vetaherah", :gloss "and she shall be pure"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Defective spelling ממקר (missing vav) yields rearranged letters מרקם; adjacent word וטהרה signals these stains are pure"}], :frame {:text "The word מקור ('source') is a euphemism for the womb and genital flow; see Leviticus 12:7 where it appears in the context of a woman's purification. The law distinguishes different types of blood-stains: a stain (כתם) found on a woman's body or clothing raises questions of impurity depending on its source, as discussed in Mishnah Nega'im 9:14 and tractate Niddah.", :sources ["Leviticus 12:7" "Mishnah Nega'im 9:14" "Niddah"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וטהרה ממקר דמיה", :translit "vetaherah mimkor dameha", :gloss "and she shall be purified from her source of bloods"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "וטהרה ממקר דמיה", :b "חמשה מיני דמים טמאים בה"}], :frame {:text "The number five types of impure bloods in a menstruating woman is a matter of tannaitic dispute. Rabbi Akiba rules that any blood visible during the days of menstruation is impure and counts specific categories; see Niddah 19a and the discussion at Niddah 33b regarding the five colors of blood that render a woman impure (Niddah 19a).", :sources ["Niddah 19a" "Niddah 33b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ממקור", :translit "mimkor", :gloss "from [her] source"} {:he "ממקור ישראל", :translit "mimkor yisrael", :gloss "from the source of Israel"} {:he "וטהרה ממקור דמיה", :translit "vetaherah mimkor dameha", :gloss "and she shall be purified from her source of bloods"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ממקור דמיה" "ממקור ישראל"]}], :frame {:text "The 'bride's blessing' (ברכת חתנים) recited under the wedding canopy is structured as a series of blessings said in the presence of ten men, as established in Ketubot 7b–8a. The link between Leviticus 12:7 and wedding liturgy hinges on reading 'וטהרה ממקור דמיה' as referring to the blood of virginity, establishing the verse's relevance to a bridegroom; this derash appears in Ketubot 8a.", :sources ["Ketubot 7b" "Ketubot 8a" "Leviticus 12:7" "Isaiah 48:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "דמיה", :translit "dameha", :gloss "her bloods"} {:he "ממקור דמיה", :translit "mimkor dameha", :gloss "from her source of bloods"} {:he "והיא גלתה את מקור דמיה", :translit "vehigalah et mekor dameha", :gloss "and she has uncovered the source of her bloods"} {:he "וגלתה הארץ את דמיה ולא תכסה עוד על הרוגיה", :translit "vegilata ha'aretz et dameha velo tchaseh od al harugeha", :gloss "and the land has uncovered its bloods and no longer covers its slain"} {:he "כי כאשר יקום איש על רעהו ורצחו נפש כן הדבר הזה", :translit "ki ka'asher yakum ish al re'ehu urtzacho nafesh ken hadavar hazeh", :gloss "for as when a man rises against his fellow and murders a soul, so is this matter"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ממקור דמיה" "והיא גלתה את מקור דמיה" "וגלתה הארץ את דמיה ולא תכסה עוד על הרוגיה"]} {:kind :other, :note "Gezerah shavah linking גילוי דמים ('uncovering of bloods') in Leviticus 20:27 to גילוי דמים in Ezekiel 24:7–8 via the shared term, with Ezekiel 24:7–8 comparing spilled blood to murder as in Deuteronomy 22:26"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'וגלתה הארץ את דמיה' in Ezekiel 24:8 refers to blood crying out from the ground, echoing the blood of Abel in Genesis 4:10. The comparison 'כי כאשר יקום איש על רעהו' quotes Deuteronomy 22:26, where the Torah compares a betrothed woman's assault to murder; this same verse is invoked in the gezerah shavah between blood of menstruation and blood of murder in Niddah 21a.", :sources ["Genesis 4:10" "Ezekiel 24:7" "Ezekiel 24:8" "Deuteronomy 22:26" "Niddah 21a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Defective spelling ממקר (missing vav) yields rearranged letters